  "notes": "A generator proposes molecules. A structure model evaluates or proposes conformations. Docking scores, learned affinity estimates and alchemical free energies have different meanings and assumptions. Boltz-2's standard affinity output concerns protein–small-molecule binding. It is not a validated universal protein–protein affinity estimator. Property flags identify concerns that need further evaluation. This table is an author synthesis of the documented roles of representative tools.\n\nSources\n[REINVENT] Loeffler et al. REINVENT 4: Modern AI-driven generative molecule design. Journal of Cheminformatics (2024)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1186/s13321-024-00812-5\nhttps://github.com/MolecularAI/REINVENT4\n\n[RF] Watson et al. De novo design of protein structure and function with RFdiffusion. Nature (2023)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06415-8\n\n[RFD4] NVIDIA and Baker Laboratory. RFD4-Proteina documentation and model card\nhttps://docs.nvidia.com/nim/bionemo/rfd4-proteina/overview\nhttps://huggingface.co/nvidia/RFD4-Proteina-Diffusers\n\n[BINDCRAFT] Pacesa et al. One-shot design of functional protein binders with BindCraft. Nature (2025)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09429-6\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12507698/\n\n[MPNN] Dauparas et al. Robust deep learning-based protein sequence design using ProteinMPNN. Science (2022)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1126/science.add2187\nhttps://github.com/dauparas/ProteinMPNN\n\n[AF3] Abramson et al. Accurate structure prediction of biomolecular interactions with AlphaFold 3. Nature (2024)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07487-w\n\n[BOLTZ] Boltz-2. Official model repository and accompanying paper\nhttps://github.com/jwohlwend/boltz\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12262699/\n\n[VINA] AutoDock Vina. Official documentation\nhttps://autodock-vina.readthedocs.io/en/latest/\n\n[OPENFE] OpenFE. Official documentation\nhttps://docs.openfree.energy/en/latest/\n\n[RDKIT] RDKit. Overview\nhttps://www.rdkit.org/docs/Overview.html\n\n[TAP] Raybould et al. Five computational developability guidelines for therapeutic antibody profiling. PNAS (2019)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1810576116\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410772/\n\nSource check: 8 November 2026. Representative tools, not an exhaustive ranking."
